{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1786439519,"nonce":"QPOTAr1Oh0lfYEbqCOQITD+YEQc=","title":"SSE resume ignores the interest filter and replays every resource","message":"**What is wrong.** `GET /api/v1/stream?resources=…` filters correctly on the LIVE path but not on the RESUME path. Reconnecting with `Last-Event-ID` replays **every** resource kind, regardless of what the client subscribed to.\n\nObserved live against a real server and a real Postgres (schema 00039):\n\n```\n# LIVE, resources=alerts — correct: one matching frame, nothing else\nGET /api/v1/stream?resources=alerts\n  event: alert.upserted        (x1, after ingesting one alert)\n\n# RESUME, resources=alerts, Last-Event-ID: 10 — WRONG: every resource replayed\nGET /api/v1/stream?resources=alerts   (Last-Event-ID: 10)\n  id: 11  event: occurrence.upserted     \u003c- not subscribed\n  id: 12  event: alert.upserted          \u003c- subscribed, correct\n  id: 13  event: event.appended          \u003c- not subscribed\n  id: 14  event: event.appended          \u003c- not subscribed\n  id: 15  event: event.appended          \u003c- not subscribed\n  id: 16  event: group.upserted          \u003c- not subscribed\n```\n\nThe same asymmetry shows the other way round: those `event.appended` frames (13-15) were **absent** from the live stream that was open when they were generated, and present on every resume — so the filter demonstrably exists and demonstrably is not applied on replay.\n\n**Why it matters.** Reconnection is not an edge case; it is what happens every time a laptop sleeps, a proxy times out a long-lived connection, or a deploy rolls a pod. So the normal steady state of a long-open alert list is: correct frames while connected, then a burst of unsubscribed frames on every reconnect.\n\nThe consequences are bandwidth and correctness rather than security — `org_id` scoping is intact on every frame I saw, and the resume is otherwise exact (from id 11 it replayed 12 onward with no duplicate and no gap, exactly as ADR 0010 claims). But a client that subscribed narrowly is entitled to assume it will not be handed kinds it never asked for, and a reducer that meets an unexpected kind is precisely the shape that produces a silently-dropped event or a rendered `undefined`. The UI's own tests cover 'unknown kind delivered and still advancing lastSeq', so the app will survive it — it should not have to.\n\n**Where.** `internal/streaming/` — the resume/backfill query path, as against the live fan-out path which applies the filter correctly. `internal/platform/httpx/sse.go`, ADR 0010.\n\n**Done when.** The replay applies the same interest filter as the live path, a resume with `resources=alerts` yields only alert frames, and the sequence contract is unchanged (resume from N still starts at N+1 with no gap — note `lastSeq` must still advance past filtered-out ids, or a client resuming after a filtered frame will ask for it forever). A test drives resume with a narrow filter across a gap containing other resources.\n\nFound by running the UI/stream stack against a real backend for issue 4892848.","files":null}]}